          "correctAnswer": 0,
          "explanation": "HPV is a <strong>virus</strong>. Chlamydia, in contrast, is caused by a bacterium."
        },
        {
          "question": "Chlamydia can be cured with...",
          "options": [
            "Antibiotics",
            "A vaccine",
            "Hormones",
            "A condom"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 0,
          "explanation": "Chlamydia is caused by a bacterium, so <strong>antibiotics</strong> can cure it. A condom protects but it does not cure."
        },
        {
          "question": "When does the HPV vaccine work best?",
          "options": [
            "Before the first sexual contact",
            "After catching the virus",
            "During menstruation",
            "Only for adults over 30"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 0,
          "explanation": "A vaccine prepares the body before it meets the virus, so it works best <strong>before the first sexual contact</strong>."
        },
        {
          "question": "What is dual protection?",
          "options": [
            "Protecting yourself against both a pregnancy and STIs",
            "Taking the pill twice a day",
            "Having two partners",
            "Getting vaccinated twice"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 0,
          "explanation": "<strong>Dual protection</strong> means protecting against a pregnancy and against STIs at the same time, for example with a condom."
        },
        {
          "question": "A new method is a hormonal patch worn on the skin. How does it most likely stop a pregnancy?",
          "options": [
            "Its hormones stop ovulation",
            "It catches sperm cells like a condom",
            "It cures STIs",
            "It blocks the urethra"
          ],
          "correctAnswer": 0,
          "explanation": "Like the pill and the implant, a hormonal method <strong>stops ovulation</strong>. A patch does not act as a barrier."
        },
        {
          "question": "Sam said yes earlier but now says no. What should the partner do?",
